data.content:
### What changed

`TimeProvider` (built-in since .NET 8) is injected via an **optional** constructor parameter, so existing callers keep working unchanged.

**In tests**, use the testing package:

```bash
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

No interfaces, no DI container, no ambient static — just one optional parameter per class.
